<h2>Claude Code on Your Own Servers &mdash; the Self-Hosting Public Beta and August's Releases</h2>

<h3>Three-line summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Claude Code's <strong>self-hosted environments</strong> &mdash; running agent sessions on <strong>your organization's own infrastructure</strong> instead of Anthropic's cloud &mdash; opened as a public beta on 6 August 2026. Team and Enterprise plans, one command (<code>claude self-hosted-runner</code>), and your machine becomes the execution layer.</li>
    <li>The same week brought <strong>GitLab merge request support</strong> (<code>--worktree</code> now accepts MR URLs) and <strong>auto-continue</strong> (a session stopped by a usage limit resumes by itself when the window resets, 14 August).</li>
    <li>The three point the same way &mdash; control over where sessions run, which platforms they target, and when they work all moves <strong>to the organization</strong>. It is the next step after the <a href="#!news-20260814-auto-mode-stateless-mcp">auto-mode default</a> moved approval decisions into written rules.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Sessions crossed into the organization's boundary</h3>
<p>Until now, a remote Claude Code session ran in an isolated container managed by Anthropic. The code is cloned fresh when the session starts and the container is reclaimed when it ends &mdash; convenient, but the plain fact that <strong>data passes through computers outside the organization</strong> made it a non-starter for organizations bound by data residency (a requirement that data never leave a specified location) or network-separation rules, as in finance, healthcare, and defense.</p>
<p>The self-hosted environments beta, opened on 6 August, moves that boundary. With the <code>claude self-hosted-runner</code> command that landed in Claude Code v2.1.224 (7 August), your machine or container becomes the session's execution layer, and the agent works <strong>next to your internal services, toolchains, and security controls</strong>. Runners operate in two modes.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Fixed</strong> &mdash; keep a set number of runners up at all times and distribute sessions across them.</li>
    <li><strong>On-demand</strong> &mdash; watch for waiting sessions, spin a runner up when work arrives, and shut it down when it finishes.</li>
</ul>
<p>It ships disabled by default and an organization administrator has to turn it on in settings; organizations on ZDR (Zero Data Retention &mdash; a contract option under which no data is stored at all) cannot use it yet. In short, this is not a feature everyone flips on &mdash; it is <strong>a path for organizations whose compliance requirements had blocked adoption</strong>.</p>

<h3>The rest of the week &mdash; GitLab MRs, auto-continue, Compliance API</h3>
<p><strong>GitLab merge request support.</strong> <code>claude --worktree</code> now takes GitLab MR URLs alongside GitHub PRs, and the agent list shows MRs as <code>!N</code>. Platform detection works from git remotes and API probing, so it catches <strong>self-hosted GitLab instances</strong>, not just GitLab.com &mdash; a change that lands directly on organizations running internal GitLab, the very environment this wiki's <a href="#!dzp-overview">AI development knowledge platform proposal</a> assumes.</p>
<p><strong>Auto-continue.</strong> A single checkbox that arrived in the desktop app on 14 August. A session stopped by a usage limit resumes automatically the moment the limit window resets. The limits themselves (the 5-hour window and the weekly cap) are unchanged &mdash; what changed is only that <strong>the restart a person used to come back for</strong> now happens on its own. For long autonomous jobs left running overnight, the difference is tangible.</p>
<p><strong>Compliance API expansion.</strong> The API that lets security teams query session content and metadata in one place for audits and eDiscovery (electronic evidence disclosure) now covers Cowork and every Claude Code surface &mdash; desktop, web, mobile, and CLI (Enterprise beta).</p>

<h3>Why this is one current</h3>
<p>This week's releases look miscellaneous but move in one direction. Where the <a href="#!news-20260814-auto-mode-stateless-mcp">auto-mode default</a> moved the question of <em>what to allow</em> from a prompt asked every time into <strong>rules written in advance</strong>, this week moved <em>where sessions run</em> (self-hosting), <em>what they target</em> (self-hosted GitLab), and <em>how long they work</em> (limit windows with automatic resumption) into values the organization sets. As agents grow stronger, the point of control shifts from the conversation to <strong>the environment and the rules</strong> &mdash; the same thread that runs through the permission editing and verification-splitting advice in this wiki's <a href="#!tip-advanced">advanced tips</a>.</p>

